    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VI of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlfonsoVI_of_Castile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded by MaiDireLollo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Portugal grew out of the County of Portugal, the land between the Minho and the Mondego that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> of León granted in the 1090s to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, husband of his daughter Teresa. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> defeated his mother’s party at the Battle of São Mamede in 1128 and took control of the county, ruling first from Guimarães and then from Coimbra. After a victory over Muslim forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ourique">Battle of Ourique</a> in 1139, whose scale was greatly enlarged by later legend, he began to call himself king. Alfonso VII of León accepted the royal title at the Treaty of Zamora in 1143, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a> as king in the bull <em>Manifestis probatum</em> of 1179. The new kingdom expanded south at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>: with the help of English, Flemish and German crusaders bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, Afonso took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lisbon">Lisbon</a> after a siege in 1147, and Santarém fell the same year. The frontier then moved into the Alentejo with the help of the military orders. Afonso III completed the conquest of the Algarve with the capture of Faro in 1249, and the Treaty of Alcañices with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1297 fixed a land border that has changed very little since, making Portugal one of the oldest stable states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#lisbon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lisbon.jpg" alt="Lisbon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#lisbon">Lisbon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisboa_-_Portugal_(52597836992).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitor Oliveira from Torres Vedras, PORTUGAL, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Portugal was a small agrarian kingdom of perhaps a million people, divided between a populous, densely farmed north of small holdings and a south of large estates held by the crown, the church and the military orders. Its towns governed themselves through charters, the <em>forais</em>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lisbon">Lisbon</a> and Porto grew into Atlantic ports exporting wine, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olive-oil">olive oil</a>, fish and cork to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. King Denis (r. 1279–1325) encouraged farming and trade, founded a university in Lisbon in 1290, later moved to Coimbra, and promoted Portuguese as the language of royal documents. The fifteenth-century voyages transformed this economy. Gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madeira">Madeira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sao-tome">São Tomé</a>, enslaved Africans, and from 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a> brought by the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#casa-da-india">Casa da Índia</a> made Lisbon one of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#entrepot">entrepôts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Brazilian sugar dominated the seventeenth century, and the gold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, found in the 1690s, flowed through Lisbon in the eighteenth. Society remained strongly Catholic and hierarchical. Manuel I forced the conversion of the kingdom’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a> in 1497, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Portuguese Inquisition</a>, founded in 1536, pursued these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-christians">New Christians</a> for two centuries. Much colonial wealth passed through Portugal to northern Europe, while the kingdom’s own manufacturing stayed weak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-alcacer-quibir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir.jpg" alt="Battle of Alcácer Quibir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lagos46_kopie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Jansoone, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early kingdom was made by war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, fought by royal hosts, town militias and the military orders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#knights-templar">Templars</a>, Hospitallers, Santiago and Avis, and later the Order of Christ, which Denis founded in 1319 to take over the Templars’ property. Its independence was tested above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. During the crisis of 1383–1385 John, Master of Avis, and his constable Nuno Álvares Pereira defeated a much larger Castilian army at the Battle of Aljubarrota on 14 August 1385, with the help of English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#archery">archers</a>, and the Treaty of Windsor of 1386 began an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> that still exists. Overseas power rested on ships and guns: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caravel">caravels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carrack">carracks</a> armed with artillery let small fleets dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> in 1509, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#goa">Goa</a> in 1510, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hormuz">Hormuz</a> in 1515. Disaster came in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morocco">Morocco</a>, where King Sebastian died with much of the nobility at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578. After 1640 the Restoration War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> was won at Ameixial in 1663 and Montes Claros in 1665. Portugal was later occupied by French armies from 1807 and defended with British help, notably behind the Lines of Torres Vedras in 1810.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#marquis-of-pombal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal.jpg" alt="Marquis of Pombal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armas_marqueses_pombal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No machine-readable author provided. Jsobral assumed (based on copyright claims)., CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Portugal was a hereditary Catholic monarchy whose kings ruled through a royal council, a chancery and royal judges, while the Cortes brought together clergy, nobility and representatives of the towns, which first sat together at Leiria in 1254. The Cortes voted taxes and acted at moments of crisis, notably at Coimbra in 1385, when the jurist João das Regras argued for the election of John I, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>. Royal law was codified in the <em>Ordenações Afonsinas</em> of the 1440s, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manueline-style">Manueline</a> and Philippine ordinances. Overseas, the crown kept trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> as a royal monopoly run through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#casa-da-india">Casa da Índia</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#goa">Goa</a>, and it divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> into captaincies from the 1530s. From 1580 to 1640 Portugal shared its kings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, though the Cortes of Tomar in 1581 guaranteed its separate laws and offices. After the Braganza restoration the Cortes ceased to meet after 1698, and gold revenues let John V govern without them. Under Joseph I the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> ran an enlightened absolutism from 1750 to 1777, reforming finance and education and expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1759. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution of 1820</a> brought constitutional rule, settled by the Constitutional Charter of 1826, which with amendments lasted until 1910.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#jeronimos-monastery"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery.jpg" alt="Jerónimos Monastery" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1502_legte_K%C3%B6nig_Manuel_I._den_Grundstein_f%C3%BCr_das_Hieronymus_Kloster_in_Lissabon._06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Holger Uwe Schmitt, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Portugal opened the sea routes that joined the continents. Under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a> its sailors settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madeira">Madeira</a> and the Azores, passed Cape Bojador in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> rounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> in 1488. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#calicut">Calicut</a> in 1498, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> in 1500, and by mid-century Portuguese traders were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494, which divided the newly found lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, shaped the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> that grew from these voyages lasted until 1999, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#macau">Macau</a> was handed to China, and left Portuguese as the language of Brazil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mozambique">Mozambique</a> and other countries, today spoken by more than 250 million people. It also opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> on a large scale: Portuguese and Brazilian ships carried more enslaved Africans across the Atlantic than those of any other nation. Portuguese culture produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manueline-style">Manueline style</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tower-of-belem">Tower of Belém</a>, the epic <em>Os Lusíadas</em> of Luís de Camões, published in 1572, and advances in navigation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cartography">cartography</a>. The alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, the oldest in force in the world, became a lasting part of European diplomacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#philip-ii-of-spain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Philip II of Spain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Philip_II_of_Spain_by_Sofonisba_Anguissola_-_002b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sofonisba Anguissola, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The death of Sebastian at Alcácer Quibir without an heir opened a succession crisis, and in 1580 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> made good his claim by invasion, beginning sixty years of union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> during which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> attacked Portuguese posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a>. A revolt on 1 December 1640 placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a> on the throne as John IV, and Spain recognised independence by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1668. Brazilian gold financed a revival, but the Lisbon earthquake of 1 November 1755 destroyed much of the capital. In 1807, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s army under Junot advanced, the royal court sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, and in 1815 the realm became the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves. Brazil declared independence in 1822, and the kingdom was then torn by the Liberal Wars of 1828–1834 between Pedro IV and his brother Miguel. Later decades of rotating parties and debt were followed by the British Ultimatum of 1890, which ended Portuguese claims between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mozambique">Mozambique</a> and discredited the monarchy. King Carlos I and his heir were assassinated in Lisbon on 1 February 1908. On 5 October 1910 a republican revolution deposed Manuel II, who went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, and the First Portuguese Republic replaced the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Portugal kept borders that have barely changed since 1297 and, from a small Atlantic base, opened sea routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a>, creating the first global maritime empire and spreading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-language">Portuguese language</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> across several continents.</em></p>

